{% doc %}
  Outputs the WhatsApp number in the form wa.me needs: digits only, including
  the country code, with no +, spaces or punctuation. Outputs nothing at all if
  no number is set.

  wa.me silently fails on a number without a country code — it does not error,
  it just opens a "phone number shared via link is invalid" page. The admin
  field was filled in as a bare 10-digit Indian mobile, so a 10-digit value is
  treated as Indian and given a 91 prefix. Anything 11 digits or longer is
  assumed to already carry its country code and is passed through untouched.

  Serves the call button too, which may dial a different number: pass one in and
  it is normalised the same way, otherwise the WhatsApp number is used. The rule
  about country codes lives here and only here, so the two buttons cannot drift.

  Note the output carries no leading +, which is what wa.me wants. A tel: link
  has to add one.

  Capture it, don't print it, when you need the value:

  @example
  {%- capture wa_number -%}{%- render 'whatsapp-number' -%}{%- endcapture -%}
  {%- capture call_number -%}{%- render 'whatsapp-number', number: settings.call_number -%}{%- endcapture -%}

  @param {string} [number] - A number to normalise instead of settings.whatsapp_number
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign source = number | default: settings.whatsapp_number
  assign wa = source | remove: ' ' | remove: '-' | remove: '(' | remove: ')' | remove: '+' | strip
  if wa == blank
    break
  endif
  assign wa_size = wa | size
  if wa_size == 10
    assign wa = '91' | append: wa
  endif
  echo wa
-%}
